1. What It Is
A provenance-aware, versioned registry of climate attribution claims. A climate attribution claim is a statement that a specific event, trend, or phenomenon is attributable, in whole or in part, to a specific cause: anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions, natural variability, volcanic activity, solar variation, land-use change, and so on. The Climate Attribution Registry records each claim with its event or trend, its attributed cause, its evidence, its models, its uncertainty, its confidence level, and its provenance.

It reuses:

the Physics Knowledge Library (App 23)

the Full-Pipeline Simulator (App 9)

the Null-Model Protocol Library (App 18)

the Provenance-Aware Annotation Database (App 20)

the PROV-O Exporter (App 3)

the Revision History Viewer (App 7)

the Numerical Verification Tool (App 22)

the Evidence Aggregator (App 46)

the Autofill Library Service (App 49)

It is for climate scientists, policymakers, journalists, students, and anyone who wants to know what is attributed to what and with what confidence.

2. Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept deposits of climate attribution claims from users.

Store each claim with its event or trend, its attributed cause, and its provenance.

Record the event or trend: the description, the period, the region, and the type.

Record the attributed cause: the cause, the mechanism, and the contribution estimate.

Record the evidence: the observations, the models, the statistical analyses, and the physical reasoning.

Record the models used: the climate models, their versions, their resolutions, and their ensemble members.

Record the uncertainty: the confidence interval, the probability, and the sources of uncertainty.

Record the confidence level: very_high, high, medium, low, or very_low.

Record the attribution method: fraction_of_attributable_risk, probabilistic_event_attribution, trend_attribution, or other.

Record the counterfactual baseline: the world without the attributed cause.

Record the revisions: how the claim has changed over time, and why.

Enforce provenance completeness.

Enforce versioning.

Enforce supersession acyclicity.

Support querying by attribution ID, event, cause, region, period, confidence level, attribution method, depositor, deposit date, and keywords.

Support full-text search of the event description, the cause, the evidence, and the notes.

Support aggregation queries: count by cause, by region, by period, by confidence level, by attribution method, by year.

Support exporting a claim as JSON, PROV-O, and Turtle.

Support importing a claim from JSON.

Support bulk import and bulk export.

Support a REST API for CRUD operations.

Support a GraphQL API for flexible queries.

Support a command-line interface for administration.

Support authentication and authorization.

Support audit logging.

Support backup and restore.

Support a web interface.

Support moderation.

Support linking a claim to a Physics Knowledge Library work if the claim is described in a deposited work.

Support linking a claim to a Full-Pipeline Simulator result.

Support linking a claim to a Null-Model Protocol result.

Support linking a claim to a Numerical Verification Tool result.

Support the attribution map: a visualisation of attributions by region and by event type.

Support the attribution confidence view: a visualisation of the confidence levels across claims.

Support the attribution timeline: a visualisation of how the attribution has changed over time.

Support the counterfactual view: a visualisation of the world with and without the attributed cause.

Support the cause comparison view: a visualisation that compares two or more causes for the same event.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic.

Fast: query a typical attribution in under 100 milliseconds.

Scalable: support up to 10^6 attributions.

Versioned: the registry records the STAG schema version, the Null-Model Protocol schema version, the attribution schema version, and the registry version.

Reproducible.

Portable.

Well-documented.

